## Purpose

This report updates the earlier grouped analysis using the new `LOADS` and `MARKS` workbooks.

The important change is that the new files contain two different units of observation:

1. `LOADS` gives the tested load-level denominator: load number, load quantity, and firearm/caliber category.
2. `MARKS` gives the observed mark-level data: load, cartridge, mark identifier, minutiae count, ESLR, log10 ESLR, and ID.

This means the two-part analysis should now be defined more carefully:

1. **NIFM occurrence:** at the cartridge/round level, did a tested cartridge yield at least one observed NIFM?
2. **Mark informativeness:** conditional on a cartridge yielding a NIFM, how informative is the mark? This can be analyzed either at the mark level or at the cartridge level using the best/maximum mark on each cartridge.

The cartridge-level version is usually the cleanest scientific target for occurrence and evidential yield, because a cartridge can have more than one mark. Counting mark rows directly would overstate the number of cartridges with NIFM.

## Data

```{r setup}
options(stringsAsFactors = FALSE)

needed_packages <- c("readxl", "dplyr", "tidyr", "knitr", "ggplot2")
missing_packages <- needed_packages[!vapply(needed_packages, requireNamespace, quietly = TRUE, FUN.VALUE = logical(1))]
if (length(missing_packages) > 0) {
  stop(
    "Please install the following packages before rendering this file: ",
    paste(missing_packages, collapse = ", ")
  )
}

library(readxl)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(knitr)
library(ggplot2)
```

```{r load-data}
data_candidates <- list(
  loads = c(
    "LOADS 4-28-26.xlsx",
    "data/LOADS 4-28-26.xlsx",
    "../data/LOADS 4-28-26.xlsx"
  ),
  marks = c(
    "MARKS 4-28-26.xlsx",
    "data/MARKS 4-28-26.xlsx",
    "../data/MARKS 4-28-26.xlsx"
  )
)

loads_path <- data_candidates$loads[file.exists(data_candidates$loads)][1]
marks_path <- data_candidates$marks[file.exists(data_candidates$marks)][1]

if (is.na(loads_path)) stop("Could not find LOADS 4-28-26.xlsx")
if (is.na(marks_path)) stop("Could not find MARKS 4-28-26.xlsx")

loads_sheet <- if ("Loads" %in% excel_sheets(loads_path)) "Loads" else excel_sheets(loads_path)[1]
marks_sheet <- if ("Marks" %in% excel_sheets(marks_path)) "Marks" else excel_sheets(marks_path)[1]

loads_raw <- read_excel(loads_path, sheet = loads_sheet)
marks_raw <- read_excel(marks_path, sheet = marks_sheet)

clean_colnames <- function(x) {
  x <- gsub("\\r|\\n", " ", x)
  x <- gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9]+", "_", x)
  x <- gsub("^_+|_+$", "", x)
  tolower(x)
}

names(loads_raw) <- clean_colnames(names(loads_raw))
names(marks_raw) <- clean_colnames(names(marks_raw))

required_load_cols <- c("round_designation", "load", "load_quantity", "category")
required_mark_cols <- c("load", "cartridge", "mark", "minutiae", "eslr", "log10eslr", "id")

if (!all(required_load_cols %in% names(loads_raw))) {
  stop("LOADS is missing one or more required columns. Found: ", paste(names(loads_raw), collapse = ", "))
}

if (!all(required_mark_cols %in% names(marks_raw))) {
  stop("MARKS is missing one or more required columns. Found: ", paste(names(marks_raw), collapse = ", "))
}

loads <- loads_raw |>
  transmute(
    round_designation = round_designation,
    load = as.integer(load),
    load_quantity = as.integer(load_quantity),
    category = as.character(category),
    type = substr(category, 1, 1),
    caliber = sub("^[AR]-", "", category)
  ) |>
  filter(!is.na(load))

marks <- marks_raw |>
  transmute(
    load = as.integer(load),
    cartridge = as.integer(cartridge),
    mark = as.character(mark),
    minutiae = as.integer(minutiae),
    eslr = as.numeric(eslr),
    log10_eslr = as.numeric(log10eslr),
    id = as.integer(id)
  ) |>
  filter(!is.na(load), !is.na(cartridge), !is.na(mark))

marks_with_category <- marks |>
  left_join(loads |> select(load, category, type, caliber, load_quantity), by = "load")

nifm_marks <- marks_with_category |>
  filter(id == 0)

id_marks <- marks_with_category |>
  filter(id == 1)

unmatched_marks <- marks_with_category |> filter(is.na(category))
if (nrow(unmatched_marks) > 0) {
  stop("Some MARKS rows have load numbers not present in LOADS.")
}
```

## Data Structure Checks

The first check is whether the denominators and observed mark rows are internally consistent.

```{r data-checks}
load_summary <- loads |>
  group_by(category, type, caliber) |>
  summarize(
    n_loads = n(),
    rounds_tested = sum(load_quantity),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |>
  arrange(category)

mark_summary <- marks_with_category |>
  group_by(category) |>
  summarize(
    observed_mark_rows = n(),
    observed_loads_with_marks = n_distinct(load),
    observed_cartridges_with_marks = n_distinct(paste(load, cartridge)),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

data_structure_summary <- load_summary |>
  left_join(mark_summary, by = "category") |>
  mutate(across(
    c(observed_mark_rows, observed_loads_with_marks, observed_cartridges_with_marks),
    ~replace_na(.x, 0)
  ))

kable(
  data_structure_summary,
  caption = "Basic structure of the new LOADS and MARKS files."
)
```

## Constructing the Round-Level Occurrence Dataset

For Part 1, the numerator is the number of unique tested cartridges with at least one NIFM. The denominator is the total number of cartridges tested, given by `load_quantity`.

This is not the same as the number of rows in `MARKS`, because a single cartridge can have multiple observed marks.

```{r occurrence-dataset}
cartridge_all_marks <- marks |>
  group_by(load, cartridge) |>
  summarize(
    n_marks = n(),
    any_nifm = as.integer(any(id == 0, na.rm = TRUE)),
    any_id = as.integer(any(id == 1, na.rm = TRUE)),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

cartridge_nifm <- nifm_marks |>
  group_by(load, cartridge) |>
  summarize(
    n_nifm_marks = n(),
    max_minutiae = max(minutiae, na.rm = TRUE),
    max_log10_eslr = max(log10_eslr, na.rm = TRUE),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

load_occurrence <- loads |>
  left_join(
    cartridge_all_marks |>
      group_by(load) |>
      summarize(
        cartridges_with_any_mark = n_distinct(cartridge),
        cartridges_with_id = sum(any_id),
        cartridges_with_nifm = sum(any_nifm),
        .groups = "drop"
      ),
    by = "load"
  ) |>
  left_join(
    cartridge_nifm |>
      group_by(load) |>
      summarize(
        total_nifm_marks = sum(n_nifm_marks),
        .groups = "drop"
      ),
    by = "load"
  ) |>
  mutate(
    cartridges_with_any_mark = replace_na(cartridges_with_any_mark, 0L),
    cartridges_with_id = replace_na(cartridges_with_id, 0L),
    cartridges_with_nifm = replace_na(cartridges_with_nifm, 0L),
    total_nifm_marks = replace_na(total_nifm_marks, 0L),
    cartridges_without_nifm = load_quantity - cartridges_with_nifm
  )

if (any(load_occurrence$cartridges_without_nifm < 0)) {
  stop("At least one load has more cartridges with marks than load_quantity.")
}

occurrence_by_category <- load_occurrence |>
  group_by(category, type, caliber) |>
  summarize(
    n_loads = n(),
    rounds_tested = sum(load_quantity),
    rounds_with_any_mark = sum(cartridges_with_any_mark),
    rounds_with_id = sum(cartridges_with_id),
    rounds_with_nifm = sum(cartridges_with_nifm),
    rounds_without_nifm = sum(cartridges_without_nifm),
    total_nifm_marks = sum(total_nifm_marks),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |>
  mutate(
    any_mark_rate = rounds_with_any_mark / rounds_tested,
    id_rate = rounds_with_id / rounds_tested,
    occurrence_rate = rounds_with_nifm / rounds_tested,
    marks_per_tested_round = total_nifm_marks / rounds_tested,
    marks_per_nifm_round = total_nifm_marks / rounds_with_nifm
  ) |>
  arrange(category)

kable(
  occurrence_by_category |>
    mutate(
      any_mark_rate = round(any_mark_rate, 3),
      id_rate = round(id_rate, 3),
      occurrence_rate = round(occurrence_rate, 3),
      marks_per_tested_round = round(marks_per_tested_round, 3),
      marks_per_nifm_round = round(marks_per_nifm_round, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Occurrence of any mark, identifiable marks, and NIFMs by category, using unique load-cartridge pairs."
)
```

## Uncertainty for the Main Cartridge Rates

Now quantify uncertainty for the main cartridge-level rates using exact binomial confidence intervals.

```{r category-uncertainty}
binom_ci <- function(successes, trials, conf.level = 0.95) {
  x <- binom.test(successes, trials, conf.level = conf.level)
  c(lower = x$conf.int[1], upper = x$conf.int[2])
}

uncertainty_by_category <- occurrence_by_category |>
  rowwise() |>
  mutate(
    nifm_lower = binom_ci(rounds_with_nifm, rounds_tested)[1],
    nifm_upper = binom_ci(rounds_with_nifm, rounds_tested)[2],
    id_lower = binom_ci(rounds_with_id, rounds_tested)[1],
    id_upper = binom_ci(rounds_with_id, rounds_tested)[2],
    any_mark_lower = binom_ci(rounds_with_any_mark, rounds_tested)[1],
    any_mark_upper = binom_ci(rounds_with_any_mark, rounds_tested)[2]
  ) |>
  ungroup() |>
  select(
    category, rounds_tested,
    occurrence_rate, nifm_lower, nifm_upper,
    id_rate, id_lower, id_upper,
    any_mark_rate, any_mark_lower, any_mark_upper
  )

kable(
  uncertainty_by_category |>
    mutate(across(where(is.numeric), \(x) round(x, 3))),
  caption = "Exact 95% confidence intervals for the main cartridge-level rates."
)
```

## Incremental Yield from Including NIFMs

Now estimate the practical gain from including NIFMs rather than relying on identifiable marks alone.

```{r incremental-yield}
overall_yield <- occurrence_by_category |>
  summarize(
    rounds_tested = sum(rounds_tested),
    rounds_with_any_mark = sum(rounds_with_any_mark),
    rounds_with_id = sum(rounds_with_id),
    rounds_with_nifm = sum(rounds_with_nifm)
  ) |>
  mutate(
    any_mark_rate = rounds_with_any_mark / rounds_tested,
    id_rate = rounds_with_id / rounds_tested,
    nifm_rate = rounds_with_nifm / rounds_tested,
    gain_difference = any_mark_rate - id_rate,
    gain_ratio = any_mark_rate / id_rate
  )

load_ids <- unique(load_occurrence$load)

set.seed(20260521)
boot_results <- replicate(2000, {
  sampled_ids <- sample(load_ids, size = length(load_ids), replace = TRUE)
  sampled <- bind_rows(lapply(sampled_ids, \(x) load_occurrence[load_occurrence$load == x, ]))
  c(
    any_mark_rate = sum(sampled$cartridges_with_any_mark) / sum(sampled$load_quantity),
    id_rate = sum(sampled$cartridges_with_id) / sum(sampled$load_quantity)
  )
})

boot_df <- as.data.frame(t(boot_results)) |>
  mutate(
    gain_difference = any_mark_rate - id_rate,
    gain_ratio = any_mark_rate / id_rate
  )

overall_yield_ci <- overall_yield |>
  mutate(
    any_mark_lower = quantile(boot_df$any_mark_rate, 0.025),
    any_mark_upper = quantile(boot_df$any_mark_rate, 0.975),
    id_lower = quantile(boot_df$id_rate, 0.025),
    id_upper = quantile(boot_df$id_rate, 0.975),
    gain_difference_lower = quantile(boot_df$gain_difference, 0.025),
    gain_difference_upper = quantile(boot_df$gain_difference, 0.975),
    gain_ratio_lower = quantile(boot_df$gain_ratio, 0.025),
    gain_ratio_upper = quantile(boot_df$gain_ratio, 0.975)
  )

kable(
  overall_yield_ci |>
    mutate(across(where(is.numeric), \(x) round(x, 3))),
  caption = "Overall yield and clustered bootstrap intervals for the gain from including NIFMs."
)
```

## Descriptive Patterns

```{r descriptive-plots}
plot_data <- occurrence_by_category |>
  mutate(category = factor(category, levels = category))

ggplot(plot_data, aes(x = category, y = occurrence_rate)) +
  geom_col() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, 1)) +
  labs(
    x = "Category",
    y = "Observed NIFM occurrence rate",
    title = "NIFM occurrence by category"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

ggplot(plot_data, aes(x = category, y = marks_per_tested_round)) +
  geom_col() +
  labs(
    x = "Category",
    y = "Observed NIFM marks per tested round",
    title = "Observed NIFM mark yield by category"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

## Part 1: NIFM Occurrence

The occurrence analysis is now a grouped-binomial model where the numerator is the number of unique cartridges with at least one NIFM and the denominator is the total number of tested cartridges.

### Global Heterogeneity Test

```{r occurrence-heterogeneity-test}
occurrence_table <- occurrence_by_category |>
  select(rounds_with_nifm, rounds_without_nifm) |>
  as.matrix()

rownames(occurrence_table) <- occurrence_by_category$category
colnames(occurrence_table) <- c("NIFM", "No NIFM")

occurrence_chisq <- chisq.test(occurrence_table)
occurrence_chisq
```

### Category-Specific Occurrence Estimates

```{r occurrence-category-estimates}
occurrence_fit_category <- glm(
  cbind(rounds_with_nifm, rounds_without_nifm) ~ 0 + category,
  data = occurrence_by_category,
  family = binomial()
)

occurrence_estimates <- occurrence_by_category |>
  mutate(
    observed_probability = occurrence_rate,
    log_odds = coef(occurrence_fit_category)
  ) |>
  select(category, rounds_tested, rounds_with_nifm, observed_probability, log_odds)

kable(
  occurrence_estimates |>
    mutate(
      observed_probability = round(observed_probability, 3),
      log_odds = round(log_odds, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Category-specific occurrence estimates for Part 1."
)
```

### Exploratory Main-Effects Model

Because there are only seven category-level rows, this model is exploratory. It asks whether the pattern can be partially summarized by firearm type and caliber.

```{r occurrence-main-effects-model}
occurrence_fit <- glm(
  cbind(rounds_with_nifm, rounds_without_nifm) ~ type + factor(caliber),
  data = occurrence_by_category,
  family = quasibinomial()
)

summary(occurrence_fit)

occurrence_fitted <- occurrence_by_category |>
  mutate(
    observed = occurrence_rate,
    fitted = fitted(occurrence_fit)
  ) |>
  select(category, rounds_tested, observed, fitted)

kable(
  occurrence_fitted |>
    mutate(
      observed = round(observed, 3),
      fitted = round(fitted, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Observed and fitted NIFM occurrence rates from the exploratory grouped-binomial model."
)
```

## Part 2: Informativeness Among Observed NIFM Cartridges

The cleanest cartridge-level version of Part 2 uses the best NIFM on each cartridge, because a cartridge can have multiple marks and identifiable marks should not drive the NIFM informativeness analysis.

This asks: conditional on a cartridge having at least one NIFM, how informative is its best observed NIFM?

```{r cartridge-level-informativeness}
cartridge_with_category <- cartridge_nifm |>
  left_join(loads |> select(load, category, type, caliber), by = "load")

cartridge_informativeness_by_category <- cartridge_with_category |>
  group_by(category, type, caliber) |>
  summarize(
    nifm_cartridges = n(),
    mean_best_minutiae = mean(max_minutiae, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_best_minutiae = median(max_minutiae, na.rm = TRUE),
    best_ge_6 = sum(max_minutiae >= 6, na.rm = TRUE),
    best_ge_8 = sum(max_minutiae >= 8, na.rm = TRUE),
    best_ge_10 = sum(max_minutiae >= 10, na.rm = TRUE),
    mean_best_log10_eslr = mean(max_log10_eslr, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_best_log10_eslr = median(max_log10_eslr, na.rm = TRUE),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |>
  mutate(
    p_best_ge_6 = best_ge_6 / nifm_cartridges,
    p_best_ge_8 = best_ge_8 / nifm_cartridges,
    p_best_ge_10 = best_ge_10 / nifm_cartridges
  ) |>
  arrange(category)

kable(
  cartridge_informativeness_by_category |>
    mutate(
      mean_best_minutiae = round(mean_best_minutiae, 2),
      median_best_minutiae = round(median_best_minutiae, 2),
      mean_best_log10_eslr = round(mean_best_log10_eslr, 2),
      median_best_log10_eslr = round(median_best_log10_eslr, 2),
      p_best_ge_6 = round(p_best_ge_6, 3),
      p_best_ge_8 = round(p_best_ge_8, 3),
      p_best_ge_10 = round(p_best_ge_10, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Cartridge-level mark informativeness, using the best mark on each cartridge."
)
```

### Three-Band Distribution Test

```{r cartridge-band-test}
cartridge_band_table <- cartridge_with_category |>
  mutate(
    band = case_when(
      max_minutiae <= 5 ~ "4_5",
      max_minutiae <= 7 ~ "6_7",
      max_minutiae >= 8 ~ "8plus",
      TRUE ~ NA_character_
    )
  ) |>
  count(category, band) |>
  pivot_wider(names_from = band, values_from = n, values_fill = 0) |>
  arrange(category)

band_matrix <- cartridge_band_table |>
  select(`4_5`, `6_7`, `8plus`) |>
  as.matrix()

rownames(band_matrix) <- cartridge_band_table$category

band_chisq <- chisq.test(band_matrix, simulate.p.value = TRUE, B = 100000)
band_chisq

kable(
  cartridge_band_table,
  caption = "Best-mark minutiae-count bands among cartridges with NIFM."
)
```

### Focused High-Minutiae Analysis

```{r cartridge-high-minutiae-analysis}
high_table <- cartridge_informativeness_by_category |>
  transmute(
    category,
    high = best_ge_8,
    lower = nifm_cartridges - best_ge_8
  )

high_matrix <- high_table |> select(high, lower) |> as.matrix()
rownames(high_matrix) <- high_table$category

high_chisq <- chisq.test(high_matrix)
high_chisq

high_fit_category <- glm(
  cbind(best_ge_8, nifm_cartridges - best_ge_8) ~ 0 + category,
  data = cartridge_informativeness_by_category,
  family = binomial()
)

high_estimates <- cartridge_informativeness_by_category |>
  mutate(
    observed_probability = p_best_ge_8,
    log_odds = coef(high_fit_category)
  ) |>
  select(category, nifm_cartridges, observed_probability, log_odds)

kable(
  high_estimates |>
    mutate(
      observed_probability = round(observed_probability, 3),
      log_odds = round(log_odds, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Category-specific probabilities that a NIFM cartridge has a best mark with at least 8 minutiae."
)

high_fit <- glm(
  cbind(best_ge_8, nifm_cartridges - best_ge_8) ~ type + factor(caliber),
  data = cartridge_informativeness_by_category,
  family = quasibinomial()
)

summary(high_fit)

high_fitted <- cartridge_informativeness_by_category |>
  mutate(
    observed = p_best_ge_8,
    fitted = fitted(high_fit)
  ) |>
  select(category, nifm_cartridges, observed, fitted)

kable(
  high_fitted |>
    mutate(
      observed = round(observed, 3),
      fitted = round(fitted, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Observed and fitted probabilities that a NIFM cartridge has a best mark with at least 8 minutiae."
)
```

## Mark-Level ESLR Analysis

The `MARKS` file also includes ESLR and log10 ESLR. Since ESLR is mark-level, this section treats individual marks as the observational unit. This is useful descriptively, but should not be interpreted as fully independent evidence because marks are clustered within cartridges and loads.

```{r mark-level-eslr}
mark_level_summary <- marks_with_category |>
  group_by(category, type, caliber) |>
  summarize(
    n_marks = n(),
    mean_minutiae = mean(minutiae, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_minutiae = median(minutiae, na.rm = TRUE),
    p_minutiae_ge_8 = mean(minutiae >= 8, na.rm = TRUE),
    mean_log10_eslr = mean(log10_eslr, na.rm = TRUE),
    median_log10_eslr = median(log10_eslr, na.rm = TRUE),
    p_id = mean(id == 1, na.rm = TRUE),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |>
  arrange(category)

kable(
  mark_level_summary |>
    mutate(
      mean_minutiae = round(mean_minutiae, 2),
      median_minutiae = round(median_minutiae, 2),
      p_minutiae_ge_8 = round(p_minutiae_ge_8, 3),
      mean_log10_eslr = round(mean_log10_eslr, 2),
      median_log10_eslr = round(median_log10_eslr, 2),
      p_id = round(p_id, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Mark-level minutiae and ESLR summaries."
)

ggplot(marks_with_category, aes(x = category, y = log10_eslr)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  labs(
    x = "Category",
    y = "log10 ESLR",
    title = "Distribution of mark-level log10 ESLR by category"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

## Combined Evidential Yield

A useful summary target is the probability that a tested cartridge yields a sufficiently informative NIFM. This combines the two stages:

\[
P(\text{informative NIFM}) =
P(\text{any NIFM}) \times
P(\text{informative} \mid \text{NIFM}).
\]

Here, informativeness is operationalized as the best observed mark on the cartridge having at least 8 minutiae.

```{r combined-yield}
combined_yield <- occurrence_by_category |>
  select(category, rounds_tested, rounds_with_nifm, occurrence_rate) |>
  left_join(
    cartridge_informativeness_by_category |>
      select(category, p_best_ge_8, p_best_ge_10),
    by = "category"
  ) |>
  mutate(
    yield_best_ge_8 = occurrence_rate * p_best_ge_8,
    yield_best_ge_10 = occurrence_rate * p_best_ge_10
  ) |>
  arrange(category)

kable(
  combined_yield |>
    mutate(
      occurrence_rate = round(occurrence_rate, 3),
      p_best_ge_8 = round(p_best_ge_8, 3),
      p_best_ge_10 = round(p_best_ge_10, 3),
      yield_best_ge_8 = round(yield_best_ge_8, 3),
      yield_best_ge_10 = round(yield_best_ge_10, 3)
    ),
  caption = "Combined cartridge-level evidential yield by category."
)

ggplot(combined_yield, aes(x = category, y = yield_best_ge_8)) +
  geom_col() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, max(combined_yield$yield_best_ge_8, na.rm = TRUE) * 1.15)) +
  labs(
    x = "Category",
    y = "P(best mark has at least 8 minutiae)",
    title = "Combined yield per tested cartridge"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

## Interpretation

The new data support the same two-part framework, but the interpretation is now more precise because the denominator is available.

The most important statistical point is that **occurrence should be modeled at the cartridge level**, using `LOADS` for the denominator and unique `load`/`cartridge` pairs in `MARKS` for the numerator. Raw rows in `MARKS` are observed marks, not tested cartridges.

The main scientific quantities are therefore:

1. `P(any NIFM)`: the probability that a tested cartridge yields at least one observed NIFM.
2. `P(informative mark | any NIFM)`: the probability that a NIFM cartridge has a best mark above a practical informativeness threshold.
3. `P(informative NIFM)`: the combined yield per tested cartridge.

The ESLR variable creates a stronger version of the Part 2 analysis because it is a direct measure of associative value. Minutiae thresholds remain useful for communication, but ESLR is closer to the evidential question.

The identifiable-vs-NIFM comparison is also now cleaner. The incremental gain from including NIFMs should be computed directly from cartridge-level yields, not inferred indirectly from NIFM-only conditional summaries.

## Limitations

These analyses remain preliminary.

1. The models above use category-level summaries, so they do not fully account for clustering within loads.
2. The mark-level ESLR analysis treats marks descriptively; formal inference should account for clustering of marks within cartridges and loads.
3. The category-level main-effects models are exploratory because there are only seven categories.
4. The choice of informativeness threshold, such as 8 or 10 minutiae, should be justified substantively.
5. ESLR is missing for some marks, so ESLR analyses should report the amount of missingness.

## Recommended Next Analysis Steps

The next version should move from category-level summaries to individual-level models:

1. A cartridge-level logistic model for whether any NIFM occurs.
2. A cartridge-level model for whether the best mark has at least 8 or 10 minutiae.
3. A mark-level or cartridge-best-mark model for log10 ESLR.
4. Random effects or clustered standard errors for load, and possibly donor/gun if those identifiers are available.
5. A combined-yield analysis that reports practical probabilities per tested cartridge.

